<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seleucia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seleucia-on-Tigris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seleucia, or Seleucia-on-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, was a city founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> around the end of the fourth century BCE. It served as one of the twin capitals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and became one of the largest cities of the Hellenistic world. It later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, as accounts of the Seleucid Empire note in discussing the loss of Mesopotamia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seleucia.jpg" alt="Seleucia" width="296" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Renato de carvalho ferreira · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ctesiphon_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Seleucia stood on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, at the confluence of the river with a major canal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, in what is now the Baghdad Governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. This position let it receive traffic from both great waterways. It was the largest and most important of the many cities named for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>, and it is distinguished from the others as Seleucia-on-Tigris. Church of the East texts call it Salīq, and the Persian name Veh-Ardashir belongs to a new eastern city that Italian excavations showed to be separate from Seleucia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a>, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a> who divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s empire, enlarged an earlier settlement and made it his capital around 305 BCE; other scholars propose foundation dates of 312 and 306 BCE. He soon moved his main capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, but Seleucia stayed an important centre of trade, Hellenistic culture and regional government. To fill it, he forced almost all the inhabitants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, except temple priests and workers, to move there. In the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE it was one of the great Hellenistic cities, comparable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and larger than Syrian Antioch. Its walls enclosed at least 550 hectares.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia">Mithridates I</a> conquered it in 141 BCE, and it became the western capital of the Parthian Empire. Tacitus described it as fully Hellenistic even then, and ancient texts claim 600,000 inhabitants and a senate of 300. In 41 BCE about 5,000 Babylonian Jewish refugees were massacred there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> burned the city in 117 CE, Hadrian ceded it back to the Parthians, and it was rebuilt in the Parthian style. The Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avidius-cassius">Avidius Cassius</a> destroyed it completely in 165.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Archaeologists rediscovered the site in the 1920s, while looking for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opis">Opis</a>. University of Michigan teams excavated from 1927, and an Italian mission from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-turin">University of Turin</a> worked there from 1964 to 1968 and from 1985 to 1989. It found a Seleucid archive building with about 30,000 seal impressions, all in a fully Greek style. The city faded into obscurity, probably abandoned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> shifted its course.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seleucia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Seleucia</b> on the Tigris, with its large Greek population, and the neighbouring royal residence of Ctesiphon were the main commercial and administrative centres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Seleucia-on-Tigris</b>, founded by Seleucus I on the Tigris north of Babylon, grew into one of the largest cities of the Hellenistic world, with a population that ancient sources put in the hundreds of thousands, and was both the empire's eastern capital and its chief commercial entrepôt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
